      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$",
